      {
        "id": "post-td-concave-fundamental-entropy",
        "kind": "postulate",
        "domain": "physics",
        "title": "Concave macroscopic fundamental entropy",
        "statement": "For each admitted material constitution $c$, the model adopts a finite scalar function\n\n$$S_c:D_c\\longrightarrow\\mathbb R\\ \\text{with values measured in }\\mathrm{J\\,K^{-1}},$$\n\non the equilibrium state domain $D_c$ of [[def-td-equilibrium-state-domain]]. This framework treats $S_c$ as primitive constitutive data: its existence and form are assumed, not derived from the word entropy, statistical mechanics, or experimental evidence.\n\nOn every specified convex region $C\\subseteq D_c$ in the dimensionless coordinates of [[def-td-equilibrium-state-domain]], $S_c$ is continuous and concave, meaning that for all $x,y\\in C$ and every $\\alpha\\in[0,1]$ ([[def-interval]]),\n\n$$S_c((1-\\alpha)x+\\alpha y)\\ge (1-\\alpha)S_c(x)+\\alpha S_c(y).$$\n\nThe domain assumptions are local to the material and region named; no unlisted coordinate tuple is presumed physically realizable. The input of $S_c$ is the represented state $\\pi_c(e)$; when the function is written using physical coordinates $(U,V,n_1,\\ldots,n_r)$, those coordinates mean the SI values whose dimensionless numerical tuple is $\\pi_c(e)$. For independent separated macroscopic systems $A,B$ admitted by [[post-td-bulk-additivity]], entropy is additive under that postulate's composition map $\\kappa_{AB}$:\n\n$$S_{A\\sqcup B}(\\kappa_{AB}(x_A,x_B))=S_A(x_A)+S_B(x_B).$$\n\nOn each separately designated open, single-phase chart in the physical coordinates $(U,V,n_1,\\ldots,n_r)$, assume the numerical representative of $S_c$ is $C^2$ in the sense of [[def-ck-euclidean-maps-and-diffeomorphisms]]: its first and second coordinate partial derivatives exist and are continuous. The partial derivative $S_U$ at fixed $V,n_1,\\ldots,n_r$ is the ordinary partial derivative in the $U$ coordinate, as in [[def-directional-and-partial-derivatives]], and is assumed finite and strictly positive. The numerical chart input uses $\\hat U=U/(1\\,\\mathrm J)$; consequently $S_U=(1\\,\\mathrm J)^{-1}\\partial S_c/\\partial\\hat U$ and has units $\\mathrm{K^{-1}}$. The other physical coordinates are similarly represented using the fixed SI units of [[def-td-si-quantities]]. No differentiability, strict concavity, or positive $S_U$ is asserted outside those charts. This item does not itself define a thermometer calibration or claim a temperature law.\n\nFor the tagged bulk family with scaling maps $\\delta_\\lambda$ and domains $B_{c,\\lambda}$ from [[post-td-bulk-additivity]], also adopt, for every admitted $\\lambda>0$ and $x\\in B_{c,\\lambda}$,\n\n$$S_{c^{(\\lambda)}}(\\delta_\\lambda x)=\\lambda S_c(x).$$\n\nThis is a bulk homogeneity assumption for tagged scaled systems, not exact finite-size scaling for every body. Lieb–Yngvason develop a different accessibility-based route in which entropy is represented from additional operational hypotheses; this framework does not claim that their derivation proves the present primitive entropy postulate.",
        "deps": [
          "def-ck-euclidean-maps-and-diffeomorphisms",
          "def-directional-and-partial-derivatives",
          "def-interval",
          "def-td-equilibrium-state-domain",
          "def-td-si-quantities",
          "def-td-system-and-permitted-exchanges",
          "post-td-bulk-additivity"
        ],
        "dependency_roles": {
          "def-ck-euclidean-maps-and-diffeomorphisms": "formulation-prerequisite",
          "def-directional-and-partial-derivatives": "formulation-prerequisite",
          "def-interval": "formulation-prerequisite",
          "def-td-equilibrium-state-domain": "formulation-prerequisite",
          "def-td-system-and-permitted-exchanges": "formulation-prerequisite",
          "def-td-si-quantities": "formulation-prerequisite",
          "post-td-bulk-additivity": "formulation-prerequisite"
        },
        "provenance": {
          "statement": "ai-altered",
          "proof": "not-applicable"
        },
        "sources": {
          "references": [
            {
              "title": "David Tong, Statistical Physics, Chapter 4 of the complete author-hosted lecture notes, §§4.3.1–4.3.4",
              "url": "https://davidtong.org/pdfs/teaching/statistical-physics/statphys4.pdf"
