        "inventory_expansion_required": false
      },
      {
        "id": "post-td-entropy-maximum",
        "kind": "postulate",
        "domain": "physics",
        "title": "Equilibrium as an attained global entropy maximum",
        "statement": "Let $\\mathcal F$ be the explicitly declared feasible endpoint set of an isolated compound whose constituents are independent separated macroscopic systems, as defined in [[def-td-isolated-compound-feasible-set]]. For this route, adopt the physical criterion that any endpoint tuple the model classifies as a stable equilibrium under these fixed totals, retained partitions, and permitted exchanges satisfies\n\n$$S_{\\mathrm{tot}}(x^{\\mathrm{eq}})=\\max_{x\\in\\mathcal F}S_{\\mathrm{tot}}(x),\\qquad S_{\\mathrm{tot}}(x):=\\sum_{i=1}^{m}S_{c_i}(x_i),$$\n\nHere $\\max$ means that $x^{\\mathrm{eq}}\\in\\mathcal F$ and $S_{\\mathrm{tot}}(x^{\\mathrm{eq}})\\ge S_{\\mathrm{tot}}(x)$ for every $x\\in\\mathcal F$, using maximum in the sense of [[def-max-min]]. The finite sum is as in [[def-finite-sum]], and the summands are those of the separated macroscopic constituents under [[post-td-concave-fundamental-entropy]]. Whenever the stable-equilibrium label is used, attainment is assumed: at least one maximizing endpoint lies in $\\mathcal F$. If $\\mathcal F$ is empty or the entropy supremum is not attained, this item supplies no equilibrium state.\n\n“Global” refers to every endpoint in this exact $\\mathcal F$, not just nearby states, states reachable by a restricted short-time dynamics, or states on a selected branch. A local maximum that is not global, or a long-lived preparation trapped by kinetic barriers, is not declared stable equilibrium by this postulate. No uniqueness or relaxation dynamics is assumed.",
        "deps": [
          "def-finite-sum",
          "def-max-min",
          "post-td-concave-fundamental-entropy",
          "def-td-isolated-compound-feasible-set",
          "def-td-thermal-contact-constraints",
          "post-td-bulk-additivity"
        ],
        "dependency_roles": {
          "def-finite-sum": "formulation-prerequisite",
          "def-max-min": "formulation-prerequisite",
          "post-td-concave-fundamental-entropy": "formulation-prerequisite",
          "def-td-isolated-compound-feasible-set": "formulation-prerequisite",
          "def-td-thermal-contact-constraints": "formulation-prerequisite",
          "post-td-bulk-additivity": "formulation-prerequisite"
        },
        "provenance": {
          "statement": "ai-altered",
          "proof": "not-applicable"
        },
        "sources": {
          "references": [
            {
              "title": "David Tong, Statistical Physics, Chapter 4 of the complete author-hosted lecture notes, §§4.3.3–4.3.4",
              "url": "https://davidtong.org/pdfs/teaching/statistical-physics/statphys4.pdf"
            },
            {
              "title": "Daniel Arovas, Lecture Notes on Thermodynamics and Statistical Mechanics, §2.9",
              "url": "https://courses.physics.ucsd.edu/2010/Spring/physics210a/LECTURES/STATMECH.pdf"
            },
            {
              "title": "Lieb and Yngvason, The Physics and Mathematics of the Second Law of Thermodynamics, §§II.F and IV.A",
              "url": "https://arxiv.org/pdf/cond-mat/9708200v2"
            }
          ]
        },
        "dependency_level": 6,
        "inventory_expansion_required": false,
        "library": "physics",
        "physical_scope": "Global stable equilibrium of an explicitly specified isolated macroscopic compound under its stated total-coordinate constraints and permitted exchanges; no claim about local maxima or dynamical relaxation is included."
      },
      {
        "id": "post-td-macroscopic-second-law",
        "kind": "postulate",
        "domain": "physics",
        "title": "Macroscopic second law for isolated compounds",
        "statement": "For every admitted closed process record $P\\in\\mathcal P_{\\mathrm{cl}}(s)$ of an isolated compound, assume its declared outer boundary is isolated throughout, its material constitution is fixed, its center-of-mass kinetic and separately tracked external-field potential energies have zero endpoint change, and both endpoint preparations are equilibrium macrostates. Include every named auxiliary and work device in the compound, and restrict to constituent splits for which [[post-td-concave-fundamental-entropy]] supplies the additive total entropy. The adopted macroscopic second law is\n\n$$S_{\\mathrm{tot}}(x^+)-S_{\\mathrm{tot}}(x^-)\\ge0,$$\n\nwhere $S_{\\mathrm{tot}}$ is the sum of the constituent entropies in [[post-td-concave-fundamental-entropy]]. The process record and the zero-change conditions are those of [[def-td-process-and-protocol]]. The process may pass through nonequilibrium configurations; this postulate compares endpoint entropies only and assigns no entropy function to those intermediate configurations.\n\nFor an admitted reversible operation in the sense of [[def-td-reversible-process]], adopt zero total entropy change. An admitted irreversible adiabatic operation may have a strictly positive entropy increase, but strict increase is not required for every irreversible operation. The postulate does not state monotonicity for an open compound, an externally driven process, a subsystem that exchanges energy with an omitted environment, or every microscopic trajectory.",
        "deps": [
          "post-td-concave-fundamental-entropy",
          "def-td-system-and-permitted-exchanges",
          "def-td-process-and-protocol",
          "def-td-reversible-process"
        ],
        "dependency_roles": {
          "post-td-concave-fundamental-entropy": "formulation-prerequisite",
          "def-td-system-and-permitted-exchanges": "formulation-prerequisite",
          "def-td-process-and-protocol": "formulation-prerequisite",
          "def-td-reversible-process": "formulation-prerequisite"
        },
        "provenance": {
          "statement": "ai-altered",
          "proof": "not-applicable"
        },
        "sources": {
          "references": [
            {
              "title": "David Tong, Statistical Physics, Chapter 4 of the complete author-hosted lecture notes, §4.3",
              "url": "https://davidtong.org/pdfs/teaching/statistical-physics/statphys4.pdf"
            },
            {
              "title": "Daniel Arovas, Lecture Notes on Thermodynamics and Statistical Mechanics, §§2.5–2.6",
              "url": "https://courses.physics.ucsd.edu/2010/Spring/physics210a/LECTURES/STATMECH.pdf"
            },
            {
              "title": "Lieb and Yngvason, The Physics and Mathematics of the Second Law of Thermodynamics, §§II.F and IV.A",
              "url": "https://arxiv.org/pdf/cond-mat/9708200v2"
            }
          ]
        },
        "dependency_level": 5,
        "inventory_expansion_required": false,
        "library": "physics",
        "physical_scope": "Endpoint entropy changes for admitted macroscopic processes of an isolated compound, including its named auxiliaries; no pointwise entropy function is assigned to nonequilibrium intermediate configurations."
      },
      {
        "id": "post-td-zeroth-law",
        "kind": "postulate",
        "domain": "physics",
        "title": "Zeroth law for the declared thermal-contact family",
        "statement": "Adopt the thermal-contact relation as an equivalence relation only within its declared contact family; it supplies no numerical temperature scale or universal calibration.",
        "deps": [
          "def-td-thermal-contact-constraints"
        ],
        "dependency_roles": {
